     The F-keys follow the Norton Commander editor's bar (F1 =
     mark, F3 = open, F4 = replace, F9 = paste) as mmee's own key
     bindings, so mark/copy/move/delete blocks, search, save and
     quit read the same in mmee here as the function keys do in
     zzeeddiitt, and the kernel consoles send the identical bytes.
     Beware that at a shell prompt ^S stops terminal output until
     ^Q is typed - the tty's own flow control.  The remaining
     function keys produce codes with no byte value and are ig-
     nored in a terminal window.

     On the output side zztteerrmm emulates a deliberate subset of the
     ANSI terminal, enough for the shell, simple full-screen
     programs and llss: CR, LF, BS, TAB (8-column stops), and the
     escape sequences EESSCC [ rr;ccHH (and ff) cursor position, EESSCC [
     nnAA/BB/CC/DD cursor motion, EESSCC [ JJ clear screen and home, and
     EESSCC [ KK clear to end of line.  There are no character at-
     tributes: bold, reverse and colour are not rendered.  Other
     escape sequences are swallowed silently.

     Up to 200 lines that scroll off the top are kept and can be
     viewed with the scrollbar on the left edge; any shell output
     snaps the view back to the live screen.  A left drag selects
     text (wrapped lines are joined, trailing blanks trimmed) and
     publishes it as the PRIMARY selection on release; a middle
     click in any terminal pastes the PRIMARY selection to the
     shell.  The window menu's CCooppyy copies the PRIMARY selection
     to the clipboard, and PPaassttee types the clipboard to the
     shell.

